  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "PIXM/wv0ZVLMt/emWHT5cUa30cw=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-20T08:46:03.642Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "Three of six screened tools have a genuine gap \u2014 Alfresco, n8n and ERPNext \u2014 and none of them withholds audit behind a paywall. Their vocabularies give them away: every Alfresco extension point is a node verb, and an n8n execution is attributed to a workflow rather than a person. Neither markets itself as a system of accountability.\n\nThe three closed cases are the reverse. Flowable named the technical-versus-business distinction in its own documentation and then shipped an immutable business audit trail. Camunda added a User Operations Audit Log in 8.9. Odoo ships field-level attribution in the free edition and forces it on in Germany.\n\nThe screening implication is that vendor honesty is a better signal than feature-name matching. A tool that says it keeps an execution trace is describing its design; a tool with a feature called \"Audit Trail\" may be offering an amendment diff viewer or a vulnerability scanner.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-20T08:46:03.642899Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-20T08:46:03.642904Z"
    }
  },
